    brief: `ASSIGNMENT: five Tier 2 STUB compound pages (tier: 2, status: stub): ghk-cu, aod-9604, hexarelin, ss-31, nad-precursors. Each stub needs a complete, verified data file and an MDX with summary, grade_rationale, optional short mechanism, and the four required sections what-it-is, evidence, regulatory, risks — 500–900 words each. Verify: GHK-Cu (Gly-His-Lys copper(II) complex; Pickart; widespread cosmetic topical use — distinguish cosmetic topical products, regulated as cosmetics, from injected use, which is unstudied); AOD-9604 (modified hGH fragment 176–191 with an added tyrosine — verify; Metabolic Pharmaceuticals' obesity trials did not show efficacy — find the published or registry record; FDA status statements and any "GRAS" claims — report only what an FDA primary source says); hexarelin (His-D-2-methyl-Trp-Ala-Trp-D-Phe-Lys-NH2 — verify; a GH secretagogue studied in humans in the 1990s, including cardiac CD36 effects; WADA S2); SS-31 / elamipretide (D-Arg-Dmt-Lys-Phe-NH2 — verify; Stealth BioTherapeutics; verify on fda.gov whether FDA granted accelerated approval (e.g. for Barth syndrome, brand name, date) — if authorised, category authorised-elsewhere with authorised_products and the FDA label; this changes the page substantially); NAD+ precursors (nicotinamide riboside, nicotinamide mononucleotide, NAD+ itself — NOT peptides: category not-a-peptide, class non-peptide, sequence null; explain why they are sold alongside peptides and why the category confusion matters; small human trials; regulatory status as supplements/novel foods in the EU (verify EFSA/EC novel-food status for NR and NMN) and the US FDA position on NMN as a dietary supplement (verify the current position). Regulatory entries for EU, NL, US, WADA at minimum for every compound.`,
