    files: ['src/content/pages/en/basics/index.mdx', 'src/content/pages/en/basics/what-is-a-peptide.mdx', 'src/content/pages/en/basics/how-signalling-works.mdx', 'src/content/pages/en/basics/pharmacokinetics.mdx', 'src/content/pages/en/basics/why-not-a-pill.mdx', 'src/data/sources/basics.yaml', 'src/i18n/en/glossary/basics.json', 'research/basics.md'],
    brief: `ASSIGNMENT: the Basics section — first-principles foundations the rest of the site builds on. section: basics; reading_level B2 for the four topic pages (but every one opens with a B1 summary), B1 for the index. Each topic page 1,500–2,500 words, ≥ 6 sources (textbook-grade facts can cite reviews, pharmacology references, regulator assessment reports, labels). Use <Diagram> generously (each with a full text equivalent). The index (template: index, order 0) introduces the section in 150–300 words and ends with <PageList section="basics" />.
1. what-is-a-peptide (order 1): amino acid → peptide bond (condensation) → chain → protein; the ~50-residue convention and why it is arbitrary (insulin at 51); primary/secondary/tertiary structure; size in daltons and what size controls (kidney filtration, membrane crossing, immune recognition); D-amino acids, cyclisation, N/C-terminal caps, fatty-acid chains — how chemists make peptides last; how peptides are made (solid-phase synthesis, recombinant expression) and what impurities each route leaves (deletion sequences, racemisation, endotoxin); why "it's a natural peptide" says nothing about safety.
2. how-signalling-works (order 2): receptors (most peptide hormones act on G-protein-coupled receptors), affinity vs efficacy, agonist/antagonist/partial agonist, second messengers and amplification (one receptor → thousands of cAMP molecules), the same receptor in many tissues → one molecule, many effects; why specificity ≠ safety (on-target effects in the wrong tissue, off-target receptors, dose-dependent loss of selectivity, growth signalling pathways shared with tumours, desensitisation/tachyphylaxis, feedback loops — e.g. GH axis negative feedback).
3. pharmacokinetics (order 3): ADME from first principles; half-life and why it drives everything (dosing interval, time to steady state ≈ 4–5 half-lives, washout, accumulation) — explained conceptually with the semaglutide example (~1 week, from the label) vs native GLP-1 (~2 minutes); how peptides are cleared (proteases, kidney), half-life extension strategies (albumin binding via fatty acids, PEGylation, Fc fusion, DAC); subcutaneous absorption; why grey-market peptides usually have no human PK data and what that means. NO dosing calculations.
4. why-not-a-pill (order 4): the actual physical reasons — stomach acid denaturation, pepsin, pancreatic proteases, brush-border peptidases, the epithelial barrier (size, charge, hydrophilicity, tight junctions), first-pass liver; typical oral bioavailability of peptides (<1–2%, cite); how oral semaglutide (Rybelsus) works around it with the absorption enhancer SNAC and what that costs (very low, variable absorption; strict fasting rules — from the SmPC); small-molecule GLP-1 agonists (e.g. orforglipron — verify current regulatory status) as the alternative strategy; why claims of oral/sublingual/nasal grey-market peptides working as well as injections are unsupported.`,
