    files: ['src/content/pages/en/evidence/index.mdx', 'src/content/pages/en/evidence/how-to-read-a-study.mdx', 'src/content/pages/en/evidence/the-translation-gap.mdx', 'src/content/pages/en/evidence/evidence-grading.mdx', 'src/content/pages/en/evidence/conflicts-of-interest.mdx', 'src/data/sources/evidence.yaml', 'src/i18n/en/glossary/evidence.json', 'research/evidence.md'],
    brief: `ASSIGNMENT: the Evidence section — the site's epistemic toolkit. section: evidence; reading_level B2 (B1 summary first); each topic page 1,500–2,500 words with ≥ 6 sources; index (template: index, order 0, 150–300 words + <PageList section="evidence" />).
1. how-to-read-a-study (order 1): the hierarchy (in vitro → animal → case report → observational → RCT → systematic review/meta-analysis) and what each design can and cannot answer; n, controls, randomisation, blinding, pre-registration; surrogate vs clinical endpoints; effect size vs p-value; absolute vs relative risk (worked example in words); preprints vs peer review; a practical checklist (<Checklist>) for reading an abstract or a headline; "n = 8 rats is not evidence of human efficacy" explained from first principles.
2. the-translation-gap (order 2): why most rodent findings die — species differences (metabolism, receptor distribution, lifespan), healthy young inbred animals, induced-injury models unlike human disease, small underpowered studies, lack of blinding/randomisation, publication bias; dose scaling: allometry and body-surface-area scaling exist as starting points for first-in-human trials, but explain why they cannot turn an animal dose into a safe or effective human dose (receptor affinity, PK, toxicity) — NO numbers, NO conversion formula, NO factors a reader could apply. Cite the quantitative evidence on translation (e.g. Hackam & Redelmeier 2006 JAMA; Ineichen et al. 2024 PLOS Biol on animal-to-human translation rates; clinical development success rates by phase, e.g. Wong, Siah & Lo 2019 Biostatistics) and the TGN1412 first-in-human disaster (Suntharalingam 2006 NEJM) as a safety-translation failure. Verify every figure.
3. evidence-grading (order 3): the site's published rubric. Explain why four axes and never one score (with the BPC-157 example: extensive preclinical work, one dominant group, no human data — a specific shape a star rating would hide), how each axis is assigned (mirror guide §7 exactly, including thresholds), the inline claim levels, the mechanism-step levels, limits of the rubric (counting studies ≠ quality; a well-characterised safety profile still has rare unknowns), how grades change (corrections log). Include <GradeRubric /> and <GradeMatrix />.
4. conflicts-of-interest (order 4): how to spot a sponsored "study", a vendor-funded review, a paid key-opinion-leader or influencer; industry sponsorship and conclusions (Lundh et al. Cochrane review — verify the current version), ghost-writing, predatory journals, citation laundering (reviews citing reviews of the same group), "research" pages on selling sites that cite real studies selectively; how to check funding and COI statements; that conflicts don't make findings false, they change how much independent confirmation you need. Never name sellers or sites.`,
