    brief: `ASSIGNMENT: the core harm-reduction pages. section: safety, template: safety, reading_level B1 THROUGHOUT (short sentences, common words), reviewed_by pending-medical-review. This is standard Dutch harm-reduction practice (as practised by e.g. Mainline and Jellinek for people who inject) applied to peptides: it saves people from abscesses and sepsis. Practical, plain, non-judgemental, no dosing, no reconstitution volumes or maths. Source every clinical statement (WHO injection-safety guidance, CDC injection safety, NHS/Thuisarts patient guidance, Dutch harm-reduction organisations, RIVM). 900–1,800 words each; index 200–400 words with <PageList section="safety" /> and a prominent link to warning-signs.
- warning-signs.json (single source of truth, schema warningSignsFileSchema in src/schemas/index.ts): groups general, injection, allergic, cardiovascular, metabolic, neurological; each item { id, sign (what the person notices, plain words), why (one sentence, the danger), action emergency|urgent|stop }. Include: anaphylaxis signs; chest pain; shortness of breath; fainting; spreading redness, red streaks, hot swelling, pus, fever/chills (infection, sepsis); severe abdominal pain spreading to the back with vomiting (pancreatitis); persistent vomiting/dehydration; signs of very low blood sugar; sudden loss of vision in one eye; severe sudden headache, confusion, weakness on one side; an erection lasting more than 4 hours; dark urine with muscle pain/weakness; yellow skin or eyes; a new or changing mole; very high blood pressure symptoms; palpitations. Accurate action levels (cite).
- safety/warning-signs (order 1, the most prominent safety page): renders <WarningSigns /> (all groups) with a short intro: call 112 for emergencies, huisarts/huisartsenpost same day for urgent, what to bring (the vial and its label), be honest with clinicians, link if-something-goes-wrong. Use <Callout type="stop">.
- safety/before-you-consider (order 2): contraindications and red lines with the mechanistic reason for each (pregnancy/breastfeeding, under 18, active or past cancer and growth-promoting/angiogenic compounds, diabetes treated with insulin or sulfonylureas with GLP-1 agonists, heart disease and blood pressure with melanocortins, eating disorders with appetite-suppressing drugs, kidney disease, history of pancreatitis, personal/family history of medullary thyroid cancer/MEN2 for GLP-1 agonists, immune suppression, blood thinners, competitive athletes); never share needles, vials or pens; never use a product that looks wrong; never stop prescribed medicines; tell your doctor. Link the relevant compound pages.
- safety/sterile-technique (order 3): aseptic handling — hand hygiene, clean surface, new sterile needle and syringe every time, alcohol swab on the vial stopper and let it dry, never touch the needle or stopper, single-use vs multi-dose vials and why multi-dose vials are a contamination risk (cite CDC), preservative-containing vs sterile water (what the preservative does; no volumes), reconstitution hygiene (gentle swirling; no foaming; no maths), storage and the cold chain (refrigeration, light, freezing; why shipping temperature of grey-market products is unknown and what heat does to peptides), when to throw a vial away (cloudy, particles, colour change, cracked, unknown age), why freeze-dried powder is not proof of sterility.
- safety/injection-safety (order 4): subcutaneous injection sites in general terms, site rotation and why (lipohypertrophy), never into inflamed, bruised or scarred skin, needle-stick prevention (no recapping), what a normal reaction looks like vs an infection (cellulitis, abscess, red streaks, fever → when to seek care), sterile abscesses and granulomas, allergic reactions.
- safety/sharps-disposal (order 5): Netherlands first — naaldencontainer from the pharmacy (apotheek), how to return it (pharmacy or municipal small chemical waste collection — verify current guidance from rijksoverheid/municipal sources/KNMP), never in household waste or plastic recycling; what to do after a needle-stick injury (wash, let it bleed, contact GP/GGD or emergency department quickly for HIV/hepatitis B/C risk assessment — verify RIVM/LCI guidance); short notes for DE, BE, UK, US with <JurisdictionCard> where useful.`,
